<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luanda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as São Paulo de Loanda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luanda is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, on the Atlantic coast. It was founded in 1575–76 by the Portuguese captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulo-dias-de-novais">Paulo Dias de Novais</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> de Loanda and became the centre of Portuguese Angola and one of the busiest slave-trading ports in the Atlantic world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> held it from 1641 to 1648, and it became the capital of independent Angola in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luanda.jpg" alt="Luanda" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iamdelcioborges · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luanda_2025_focused_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom was also a major producer and exporter of cloth, to the interior and to the Portuguese at <b>Luanda</b>, where thousands of metres of Loango cloth were imported in the early seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The ‘20. and odd’ Africans landed in English Virginia in August 1619 were probably captives from Portuguese campaigns against Ndongo, shipped from <b>Luanda</b> on a slave ship bound for Veracruz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The intensification of the Atlantic slave trade aggravated internal conflicts, as rival factions competed for control of trade routes and access to European goods, while the founding of Portuguese Angola at <b>Luanda</b> in 1576 created a hostile neighbour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese and later Brazilian merchants became its principal organisers, drawing captives above all from West Central Africa through <b>Luanda</b> and other ports; Portuguese and Brazilian vessels are estimated to have carried some 5.8 million enslaved Africans across the Atlantic between the sixteenth and mid-nineteenth centuries, the majority to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Dutch–Portuguese war, a Dutch West India Company fleet under Cornelis Jol captured <b>Luanda</b> in August 1641, aiming to secure enslaved labour for the sugar plantations of Dutch Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-angola">Dutch Angola</a></li><li><blockquote>Njinga ruled with the support of Imbangala warriors, trading captives to the Dutch during their occupation of <b>Luanda</b> and later to the Portuguese after the peace of 1656.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-matamba">Kingdom of Matamba</a></li></ul></section>
